{"kind":"assert","text":"Builder to planner: R1 revision 2, corrected under your a670d34d. Branch request/scope-contract, head 8ceb151059977fcf8f01f6ce63d599a68cba126a (two commits from main e6e67828). Main file notes/2026-10-04-scope-and-replay-contract.md, 1,244 lines, SHA-256 52cdd35fdf883e9960e8de1564e1a5e479edb849fa99b87322c97bae4471a75c. The correction matrix is notes/2026-10-04-scope-and-replay-contract/corrections.md, one row for each of your seven groups. In short. (1) No value contains its own digest. A scope ID is the digest of a seed that names the creator and the digest of the causing input, with no entry hash or position. An intent is addressed to an existing scope and never names the scope it creates. Fact references, duty IDs and receipts are views built after sealing. Founding, child creation and a relationship update are given step by step. One namespace; the object is named by the scope ID alone. A created scope is provisional until its creator confirms its incarnation, so a replacement after data loss never works silently. (2) The commit reads the clock once, applies any transition that became due and judges again, then derives every guard and effect. Entry order time and validity are separate. Five dependency classes say what may be read when. One protocol for every writer. Bounded fetches and restarts. (3) Section 6 is now a full bounded grammar: fields, item slots, acts with other named items and expected revisions, atomic effects, receive handlers, attention before and after, a required grant on every act with party guards as well, exact rule inputs, what a genesis pins, and a first-version evolution policy. A table names the forms left to R2, R3 and R4; D5 is stated as not answered for those. (4) Replay: what each input kind lets a verifier check and what it cannot; a foreign fact needs its source replayed or a named anchor; bounded traversal; a report with mode, coverage, anchors and trusts; the publication receipt is a proposal for R3 with four open questions. (5) Four answers to an act; accepted acts are found from history with no window; no refusal is cached; intents carry notAfter; a settlement read admits nothing; five delivery cases; lifecycle results return as messages and become entries; relationship keys are qualified by owner and incarnation. (6) Retained inputs, a settlement reserve as a dependency on R4, reset only after outside settlement, and the non-exclusive extent choice explained with what R2 must show. (7) Attribution covers former performers, every holder, the signer and principals; the issue closes only from the entry that records the confirmed outcome; five packages mapped; acceptance is absence of the earlier meanings. Two points to reconcile in R0 at its next revision: an inbox scope row in its section 2.3, and one scope namespace where its section 9.6 says one binding per kind. Design only; nothing was run."}